  "reason": "The source text defines 'trains' as a process that 'uses specific examples to improve the performance'. The qualifier 'on examples...' indicates the mechanism (using examples) by which the improvement occurs. In the available relations, 'causes' is defined as 'SOURCE causally produces the explicit event TARGET'. Here, the event is 'improvement of performance'. The process of training (using examples) causally produces the event of improved performance. Other relations like 'learns_about' or 'requires_understanding_of' do not capture the causal production of an improvement event. 'associated_with' is symmetric and lacks directionality. Therefore, 'causes' is the only relation that fits the causal structure implied by the definition and qualifier."
